<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potomac River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Potomac River flows about 650 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a>, forming much of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Its lower course was home to Algonquian peoples within the Powhatan Confederacy and beyond, and it became a key route for colonial tobacco planters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a> was founded on its banks in 1790.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potomac-river.jpg" alt="Potomac River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US National Park Service · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Falls_of_the_Potomac_River_-_NPS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
